    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0176" n="164" sameAs="frus:frus1945v04/pg164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Bulgaria)/2–2845:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-28T13:00:00+03:00">February 28,
                            1945—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 1:33 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>580. ReDepts 279, February 9, 5 p.m.<anchor n="81" xml:id="d149-f1"/> I have
                    received a letter from Vyshinski dated February 26 in reply to Kennan’s letter
                    to the <pb facs="0177" n="165" xml:id="pg165"/> Foreign Office requesting
                    permission for a weekly Air Transport Command flight from Bari to Budapest via
                    Athens, Sofia, Belgrade and Bucharest. Vyshinski says that because of war
                    conditions and to avoid any misunderstandings or accidents the Soviet Command
                    thinks it necessary to maintain the present procedure, whereby flights of
                    American planes carrying service personnel or official mail are individually
                    cleared with the Soviet Command on the basis of the established procedure.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d149-f1" n="81" xml:id="d149-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it instructed the Embassy to endeavor urgently to obtain blanket
                    permission for a weekly American Air Transport Command plane to fly from Bari to
                    Athens, Sofia, Belgrade, Bucharest, Budapest, and return carrying official
                    passenger traffic including American diplomatic couriers and official mail
                    (740.00119 Control (Bulgaria)/2–945).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
